The file format is as follows: id SEP title SEP pinned_flag SEP body, where SEP is
 "\n---\n". The filename is {id}.note.

Implementation of rename: load the file at dir/{old_id}.note, parse it, rebuild the
 content with the new id and new title, write it to dir/{new_id}.note, and delete
 the old file. Need to keep pinned unchanged, and also preserve body and tags (tags
 are derived from body at load time, so if body doesn't change, tags don't change
 either).

Edge cases: if the old file doesn't exist, it should be an error (io error). Use std
::io::Error::new(ErrorKind::NotFound, ...).

Implementation:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)
        .map_err(|e| e)?; // read_to_string already returns io::Result, so this is
 fine
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            format!("malformed note file {}", old_path.display()),
        ));
    }
    let pinned_flag = parts[2];
    let body = parts[3];
    let new_content = format!(
        "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
        new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned_flag, SEP, body
    );
    fs::write(dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id)), new_content)?;
    fs::remove_file(&old_path)
}
```

Note: if old_id == new_id, we'd be overwriting before deleting... write the same path
, then remove_file — that would delete the note. Should we handle this? The task says
 "rename to new_id", and in the typical case old != new. But if they're identical
, it's safer to delete only when the paths differ. To be safe, I'll add that guard
.

Also, note that load_all reads every file in the directory regardless of extension
, but that's fine.

